config SECURITY_FORTRESS
	bool "Fortress fail-closed application guard"
	depends on SECURITY && SECURITYFS && SECURITY_NETWORK
	depends on NETFILTER && INET
	default n
	help
	  Fortress OS guard: an in-kernel, fail-closed policy for Android
	  application uids. It blocks app processes from launching without
	  an active policy entry, confines all app egress traffic to policy
	  tunnel interfaces (VPN kill switch without a userspace daemon),
	  and isolates apps from each other over AF_UNIX sockets.

	  "fortress" must also be listed in CONFIG_LSM. With this option
	  enabled and the LSM missing from the list, the kernel panics.

config SECURITY_FORTRESS_DEVELOP
	bool "Fortress development mode (NOT for release builds)"
	depends on SECURITY_FORTRESS
	default n
	help
	  Allows enforcement to be switched off with the "fortress.enforce="
	  boot parameter and /sys/kernel/security/fortress/enforce. In
	  permissive mode every would-be denial is only logged.

	  Release builds must say N: enforcement is then compiled in and
	  cannot be disabled at all.

config SECURITY_FORTRESS_DEVELOP_PERMISSIVE
	bool "Fortress boots permissive by default"
	depends on SECURITY_FORTRESS_DEVELOP
	default n
	help
	  Boot in permissive (log-only) mode unless "fortress.enforce=1" is
	  given. Intended for bring-up on a userspace that does not load a
	  Fortress policy yet.
